WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.912
Step four collects the second
week, as of November sixteenth.

2
00:00:03.912 --> 00:00:06.951
The same two incidents,
and now both are closed.

3
00:00:06.951 --> 00:00:10.264
The Cloud SQL incident
ended on November ninth,

4
00:00:10.264 --> 00:00:14.762
at thirteen hundred U T C, an hour
after the first week's read saw it open.

5
00:00:14.762 --> 00:00:19.674
That's the honest limit of a weekly
read: it sees an incident after the fact.

6
00:00:19.674 --> 00:00:24.693
The weekly read is for owning and closing
notices, not for detecting incidents,

7
00:00:24.693 --> 00:00:28.013
which still reach the desk
as calls and alerts first.

8
00:00:28.013 --> 00:00:30.624
Step five groups both weeks by kind.

9
00:00:30.624 --> 00:00:33.100
Four records, and every one an incident.

10
00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:35.150
That's not the fixtures being thin.

11
00:00:35.150 --> 00:00:38.663
It's the A P I: its
category has one value.

12
00:00:38.663 --> 00:00:43.298
Maintenance, retirements and security
notices never come through this A P I,

13
00:00:43.298 --> 00:00:47.780
whatever the project's estate looks
like, and that's the gap you write down.
